{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI never expected a country to feel so simultaneously ancient and futuristic—but Japan did. In Kyoto, I wandered through Fushimi Inari’s endless red torii gates, the scent of incense curling in the morning mist. Monks chanted softly as tourists paused, silent, awed. I ate matcha mochi from a stall run by a grandmother who smiled without words.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of light and motion. Shibuya Crossing surged like a river of people, yet no one bumped. I ate ramen at a tiny counter in Shinjuku, slurping noodles as the chef nodded in approval. In Akihabara, neon signs blinked anime heroes above robots that bowed politely. I bought a Hello Kitty keychain—not for the souvenir, but because the clerk wrapped it in origami paper with such care it felt sacred.\n\nI took the Shinkansen to Nara. Deer wandered freely, bowing for snacks, their eyes calm and knowing. I fed one a cracker; it licked my palm gently, as if thanking me for remembering it was alive.\n\nAt night, I sat on a ryokan balcony, sipping sake under a sky dusted with stars. The silence was thick, peaceful. No alarms, no ads—just the whisper of wind through bamboo.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers. And if you listen, it speaks volumes.\n\nI left with a heart fuller than my suitcase. No grand souvenirs—just the taste of miso soup at dawn, the quiet hum of a temple bell, and the memory of a deer’s breath on my fingers.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan. I was gently invited in.\n\nAnd I’ll go back. Not to see more—but to feel more.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", 